# Research brief

## Frozen objective (experiment-repo issue #1, read in full)

> Monte Carlo estimate of pi and how its error scales with the number of samples.
>
> Estimate pi by sampling points uniformly in the unit square and counting those inside the quarter circle. For each N in 10^3, 10^4, 10^5, 10^6 and 10^7, run 200 independent estimates with `numpy.random.default_rng` seeded from 2026 plus the run index, and record the mean absolute error against `math.pi`.
>
> Fit log(mean absolute error) against log(N) by least squares and give the exponent with a 95% interval; compare it with the -1/2 expected for Monte Carlo.
>
> Report: `report/report.md`, one page with the table of N and mean absolute error, the fitted exponent with its interval, and one log-log figure of the error with the fit.

## Supplied material and interpretation

The issue contains the full supplied material: no attachments, datasets, articles, or reference figures are provided. README.md only identifies the role. The data must be generated by the specified simulation; no external dataset is needed. There are five N values and 200 estimates per N (1,000 estimates total). Use zero-based run indices 0 through 199, hence seeds 2026 through 2225, and state this convention.

For each run, generate pairs (x,y) uniformly in [0,1)^2 and count x*x+y*y <= 1; estimate pi by 4 times the hit fraction. Reusing each specified seed across N induces dependence across sample sizes, while runs remain independent. A single nested point stream per run, with cumulative counts at the five N, reproduces restarting the same seed for every N when pairs are drawn in consistent order. Chunking must preserve this order.

Fit natural-log mean absolute error versus natural-log N with unweighted ordinary least squares, including an intercept. The expected exponent is -1/2: the hit count is binomial with probability pi/4 and the estimator standard deviation is sqrt(4*pi-pi*pi)/sqrt(N); its asymptotic mean absolute error is sqrt(2/pi) times that standard deviation. These are analytic benchmarks, not measured results.

A 95% percentile bootstrap interval over independent run indices, keeping each run's five errors together, will quantify simulation uncertainty in the exponent without treating the five correlated averages as independent observations. Also report the conventional five-point OLS t interval (3 residual degrees of freedom) as a diagnostic, explaining its different assumptions; the review owner requests both intervals in the one-page report. Use at least 10,000 bootstrap replicates, record its seed, and check endpoint stability with a second seed. Record per-N standard errors and benchmark ratios. Numerically check nested/restarted pair-stream count equivalence at all five N for at least one seed. Check bootstrap stability and compare the observed errors to the asymptotic benchmark. The measured results must determine the conclusion, including any discrepancy with -1/2.

